# Prime7 orchestration (quality-first hybrid)

Details: `docs/ai-development/ORCHESTRATION.md` (workflow and gates), `docs/ai-development/MODEL-USAGE-POLICY.md` (models).
Agents live in `.cursor/agents/`.

**Priorities, in order:** correctness and data integrity, then security and tenant isolation, business-workflow accuracy,
maintainability, tests and regression prevention, performance and UX, and finally efficient use of allowances.
Never trade quality for allowance.

**This rule is an instruction, not platform enforcement.** Cursor enforces only the agent-file `model` and `readonly`
fields, its approval prompts and your dashboard spending limits. It does not enforce gate order, loop limits or billing.

## Modes (classify at the start; say the mode and why; escalate when risk appears; if unsure, pick the higher)

- **STANDARD (default):** routine and moderately complex work. The Auto parent coordinates, and Auto handles exploration,
  routine code, docs and basic tests. Invoke Premium specialists when complexity or risk justifies them. Substantial
  changes need independent QA; security-sensitive changes need independent security review.
- **ADVANCED:** new modules, architectural changes, complex integrations, major UI redesigns. The flow is
  `architect-premium`, then `plan-auditor-premium`, then Premium developers for complex parts and Auto developers for
  routine parts, then `test-runner`, then `qa-engineer-premium`, plus security review for sensitive parts.
- **CRITICAL:** finance, accounting, inventory valuation, posting, tenant isolation, authorization, migrations. Premium
  architecture and Premium plan verification are mandatory; Premium implementation is preferred for complex logic.
  `test-runner`, `qa-engineer-premium` and `security-auditor-premium` are mandatory. Acceptance criteria must be
  evidence-backed and covered by regression tests. Block completion while critical defects remain.

## Standing Premium authorization (granted by the user on 2026-11-06)

- Premium agents (`*-premium`) may be invoked **without asking each time** for local development, planning, verification,
  debugging, testing and security review, when complexity, risk or independent verification justifies it.
- Name the Premium agents used and the reason in the final report. Do not run agents just to use allowance.
- **Not covered:** enabling on-demand billing, raising spending limits, buying credits, production deploys, destructive
  migrations, and production data changes. Those always need the user. Respect Cursor's spending controls.
- If a Premium call fails (for example a limit was reached), do not work around it. For CRITICAL work, stop and report.

## Gates and limits

- Never let the implementer be the only reviewer. A reviewer never reviews its own work. Code generation is not proof.
- Test execution (`test-runner`) is separate from QA judgement (`qa-engineer` / `qa-engineer-premium`). A test passed
  only if executed output is visible in this chat.
- Read-only agents cannot run shell; pass them `git status`, `git diff` and test output in the prompt.
- Allow at most **two** automatic correction rounds per failed gate, then report to the user.
- Run at most **two** implementation agents at once, and only in separate Git worktrees with disjoint files. Read-only
  reviewers may run in parallel. Never allow overlapping edits in one checkout.
- Ask the user before destructive actions, production migrations or deploys, force pushes, or production data changes.
- Never claim a subagent ran without a Task result in this chat.
